`aria-label` \u2014 **redundancy/conflict** \u26a0\ufe0f\n- Label is hidden with `display: none` \u2014 this removes it from the accessibility tree entirely, making the `htmlFor` association useless. The input relies solely on `aria-label=\"Search query\"` for its accessible name. This means the visible \"Search\" text (which sighted users see... wait, no \u2014 the label is hidden with display:none). Actually, let me re-read: `.search-container label { display: none; }`. So the label is completely removed from AT. The input's only accessible name comes from `aria-label=\"Search query\"`. This isn't a conflict per se (since the label is removed), but it IS redundant code \u2014 why have both if one is hidden?\n- Results use `<ul>` and `<li>` for list structure \u2713\n- Headings: h2 \u2192 h3 hierarchy. If there's no h1 in scope, this skips levels \u26a0\ufe0f\n- No landmark regions (`<main>`, `<nav>`, etc.) \u2014 but this might be a component within a larger page, so not necessarily an issue at the component level\n\n## Phase 3 \u2014 ARIA Pattern Compliance Audit\n- `role=\"status\"` with `aria-live=\"polite\"` and `aria-atomic=\"true\"`: This is correct for status messages. The WAI-ARIA APG recommends using `role=\"status\"` (which implies `aria-live=\"polite\"`) or a plain element with `aria-live`. Using both role=\"status\" AND aria-live=\"polite\" is redundant but not harmful \u2014 role=\"status\" already implies polite.\n- No custom widgets here, so no complex ARIA patterns to validate\n\n## Phase 4 \u2014 Focus Management Review\n- After form submission (search), focus remains on the submit button. There's no movement of focus to results or any announcement that content has changed beyond the live region. This is a design gap \u2014 keyboard users may not know where to look next, though the live region should announce \"Found X results.\"\n- No skip link within this component (but might exist at page level)\n- Tab order: form \u2192 input \u2192 button \u2192 (results if present). Logical \u2713\n\n## Phase 5 \u2014 State Communication Audit\n- Loading state: announced via `role=\"status\"` + visual `<p className=\"loading\">` \u2713\n- Results found: \"Found X results\" announced via live region \u2713 AND visually shown as heading + list \u2713\n- No results: \"No results found\" in live region \u2713 AND visually shown \u2713\n- **Potential issue**: The live region content changes based on conditional rendering. When `isLoading` goes from true to false, the div's children change from `{isLoading && 'Searching...'}` (which renders nothing when isLoading is false) to `{!isLoading && results.length > 0 && ...}`. With `aria-atomic=\"true\"`, the entire content should be announced as a unit. However, there's a subtle issue: when transitioning from \"Searching...\" \u2192 \"Found X results\", React re-renders the div with new children. The aria-live region should announce this change. But if both conditions could briefly render nothing (e.g., during state transitions), there might be moments where the live region is empty and then repopulates \u2014 which could cause missed announcements or double announcements.\n\nActually, let me trace through more carefully:\n1. Initial: query='', results=[], isLoading=false \u2192 live region renders `{!isLoading && results.length === 0 && query}` = `false` (query is '') \u2192 nothing rendered in div\n2. User types \"test\", submits: setIsLoading(true), setResults([]) \u2192 live region renders `{isLoading && 'Searching...'}` = `'Searching...'` \u2713 announced\n3. After timeout: setResults([...]), setIsLoading(false) \u2192 live region renders `{!isLoading && results.length > 0 && ...}` = `'Found 3 results'` \u2713 should be announced\n\nThis seems correct with aria-atomic=\"true\". The transition from \"Searching...\" to \"Found 3 results\" should work because the div's text content changes and aria-live will announce it.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtle issue. When `isLoading` is true, only `{isLoading && 'Searching...'}` renders (the other conditions are false). When isLoading becomes false AND results.length > 0, then `{!isLoading && results.length > 0 && ...}` renders. The div goes from containing \"Searching...\" to containing \"Found 3 results\". With aria-atomic=\"true\", the whole thing is announced as one unit. This should work correctly in most screen readers.\n\nHowever, there's a potential issue with `aria-atomic` and React: if the live region briefly becomes empty during re-render (between removing \"Searching...\" and adding \"Found X\"), some AT might announce an empty string or miss the transition. But since all conditions are evaluated synchronously in one render pass, this shouldn't happen \u2014 React renders once with the new state.\n\n## Phase 6 \u2014 Multi-Perspective Review\n\n**Screen reader user:**\n- Input has `aria-label=\"Search query\"` but no visible label (label is display:none). The aria-label provides a name \u2713\n- Live region announcements should work for loading, results found, and no results states \u2713\n- Results are in a semantic list with headings \u2014 screen reader can navigate by heading or list item \u2713\n- **Issue**: No landmark structure. If this component doesn't have `<main>` or other landmarks around it at the page level, screen reader users navigating by landmark won't find the search region easily. But this might be handled at the page level.\n\n**Keyboard-only user:**\n- Tab to input \u2192 type \u2192 tab to button \u2192 Enter/Space to submit \u2713\n- After submission, focus stays on button \u2014 no movement to results or skip link \u26a0\ufe0f\n- No Escape key handling (not needed for search form)\n- Focus indicator: depends on browser default; CSS doesn't customize it. The input has `border: 2px solid #0066cc` always, so focus might not be visually distinct enough \u26a0\ufe0f\n\n**Low vision user:**\n- Layout should reflow at 200% zoom (max-width: 600px with flexbox) \u27