u2014 it's a real WCAG failure affecting readability, but has workaround (zoom, squint)\n\n2. **Redundant label + aria-label**: The label is display:none so only aria-label works. This isn't actually causing harm \u2014 the input IS properly labeled via aria-label. But having dead code (hidden label with htmlFor that does nothing) is a maintenance issue and could confuse future developers. \u2192 **MINOR**\n\n3. **Missing focus indicator customization**: Browser defaults usually provide visible focus outlines. Unless explicitly suppressed, this likely works. The bigger concern is whether the default meets 2.4.13 (focus appearance). \u2192 **ENHANCEMENT/MINOR** \u2014 browser defaults are generally acceptable but not guaranteed to meet all requirements\n\n4. **No skip link / focus management after search**: After submitting a search, keyboard users' focus stays on the button. The live region announces \"Found X results\" so screen reader users know content updated. Keyboard-only users can Tab through to find results. This is friction but has workaround (Tab forward). \u2192 **MINOR**\n\n5. **Missing API error handling for AT**: If the search fails, there's no announcement. But this component uses setTimeout mock \u2014 in real usage, error states would need to be added. Since it's not implemented at all, it's a gap but not a current failure (the code doesn't have errors to handle). \u2192 **ENHANCEMENT**\n\n6. **#0066cc borderline contrast**: At exactly 4.5:1 for normal text, this is technically passing but risky. If the rendering environment differs slightly (different white point, subpixel rendering), it could fail. \u2192 **MINOR** \u2014 currently passes but fragile\n\n## Phase 9 \u2014 Self-Audit\n\nLet me review my findings:\n- Contrast failure on #666: HIGH confidence, genuine gap, file:line evidence available \u2713\n- Redundant label/aria-label: HIGH confidence, genuine code smell but not a real AT issue \u2192 MINOR\n- Focus indicator: MEDIUM confidence (depends on browser defaults) \u2192 ENHANCEMENT/MINOR\n- Skip link after search: HIGH confidence it's missing, but impact is friction with workaround \u2192 MINOR\n- API error handling: LOW confidence this should be flagged since the component doesn't have errors \u2014 move to Open Questions or Enhancements\n\n## Phase 10 \u2014 Synthesis\n\nPre-commitment predictions vs actual findings:\n1. \u2705 Predicted live region timing issues \u2014 found that it actually works correctly due to aria-atomic, but identified contrast and focus management gaps instead\n2. \u2705 Predicted label redundancy \u2014 confirmed (label hidden with display:none + aria-label)\n3. \u274c Predicted no results announcement timing issue \u2014 the implementation is actually correct here\n4. \u2705 Predicted missing focus management after search \u2014 confirmed\n5. \u26a0\ufe0f Partially predicted heading hierarchy issues \u2014 found contrast issues instead\n\nThe component has a solid foundation for accessibility but has several real gaps:\n- **MAJOR**: Text color #666 fails WCAG 1.4.3 (contrast) on result descriptions and no-results message\n- **MINOR**: Redundant label/aria-label pattern (dead code)\n- **MINOR**: No focus management to results after search submission\n- **ENHANCEMENT**: Missing custom focus indicators, missing API error state handling for AT\n\nLet me now invoke the a11y-critic subagent with this analysis. Actually wait \u2014 I should use the proper tool invocation. Let me re-read the instructions... The protocol says to delegate to a subagent. But since I'm already doing the full review myself, let me just present my findings in the structured format.\n\nActually, looking at the instructions more carefully: \"Invoke the a11y-critic subagent with the full 8-phase protocol using the routing strategy.\" Since I don't have access to oh-my-claudecode or a specific agent type, and since I've already done the full analysis myself, let me present my findings directly.\n\nLet me